  "slug": "galaxy-clustering-cc66",
  "summary": "New TNG, HR4, HESTIA and DESI-BGS mock studies quantify n(z), lens-multipole and assembly-bias effects that matter for Euclid- and DESI-era cosmology.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of simulations to quantify systematics and secondary dependencies linking observables to cosmological parameters. For nuisance modelling, on-the-fly Gaussian Process reconstruction of n(z) in Euclid-like 3×2pt analyses shows that redshift-distribution systematics bias w0wa. IllustrisTNG ellipticals indicate that multipole mass terms bias time-delay H0 by 1–6%. For secondary bias, TNG300 shows that the unbound matter fraction in the circum-halo medium largely explains halo secondary bias through cold tidal and hot inertial accretion regimes. Luminosity-weighted marked correlations are proposed as a null test for galaxy assembly bias and are tested on DESI-BGS mocks at 0.1<z<0.4. Direct observation–simulation comparisons find that SDSS genus topology matches modern HR4 galaxy models far better than earlier models, and that HESTIA underpredicts the observed Milky Way excess of close satellite pairs. Peripheral items include a GR test from DESI Legacy Imaging cluster clustering, Gini-index BCG dominance showing no evolution, and Euclid Q1 radio-loud AGN fractions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
